//? Title: Histogram equalization: a discrete CDF map, not a uniformity guarantee
//? Subtitle: Actual counts, grayscale views and quantization counterexamples
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Remap a grayscale image whose intensities occupy a narrow range, while
preserving the order of intensity levels. A global cumulative histogram supplies
a monotone lookup table, but discrete pixel masses cannot be split into an
arbitrary uniform distribution. This report follows a four-pixel calculation,
retains the original 64×64 low-contrast pattern, and displays both actual image
values and coarse histograms on shared scales. A rare-level example shows
quantization merging distinct inputs, while constant/empty and clamping controls
define the boundaries. The implementation is a global grayscale transform, not a
local adaptive method or a guarantee of improved visual information.
)

\toc

> Four pixels: count, accumulate, map

Use a 2×2 image with rows (100,100) and (101,102). The occupied counts are 2,1,1
and inclusive cumulative counts are 2,3,4. With N=4 and cdf_min=2, the
normalized values at these levels are 0,1/2,1, mapped to 0,128,255 by the
library's half-up conversion. The output rows are (0,0) and (128,255).

The following table and concept diagram use the actual histogram, CDF, mapping
and mapped output from one shared execution. Unoccupied levels are included in
the lookup table, but they contribute no output pixels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/equalization_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t =
        dm::algorithms::histogram_equalize_trace({{100, 100}, {101, 102}});
    std::print("| input level | input count | inclusive CDF | actual mapping | output count at mapped level " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int v : {99, 100, 101, 102, 255})
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", v, t.histogram[v], t.cdf[v],
                   t.mapping[v], t.output_histogram[t.mapping[v]]);
    std::print("\nPixels {}; cdf_min {}; actual output rows [{},{}] / [{},{}]; identity {}.\n",
               t.pixels, t.cdf_min, t.image[0][0], t.image[0][1], t.image[1][0],
               t.image[1][1], t.identity_mapping);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.5;
    style.height = 2.7;
    style.font_size = 22;
    d.node("input", {0, 0}, "Input pixels\n100 100\n101 102", style).node(
        "cdf", {7, 0},
        std::format("Actual CDF\n100: {}\n101: {}\n102: {}", t.cdf[100],
                    t.cdf[101], t.cdf[102]), style).node(
        "output", {14, 0},
        std::format("Actual output\n{} {}\n{} {}", t.image[0][0], t.image[0][1],
                    t.image[1][0], t.image[1][1]), style).edge("input", "cdf",
                                                               "count").edge(
        "cdf", "output", "map").text({7, 3},
                                     std::format("CDF minimum {} of {} pixels",
                                                 t.cdf_min, t.pixels), 25).save_svg("build/equalization_tiny.svg");
    if (t.image != dm::algorithms::EqualizationImage{{0, 0}, {128, 255}})
        throw std::runtime_error("tiny map");
}
```
