| input level | input count | inclusive CDF | actual mapping | output count at mapped level |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 99 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 |
| 100 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |
| 101 | 1 | 3 | 128 | 1 |
| 102 | 1 | 4 | 255 | 1 |
| 255 | 0 | 4 | 255 | 1 |

Pixels 4; cdf_min 2; actual output rows [0,0] / [128,255]; identity false.
\image(figures/equalization_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Counts and lookup entries come from the producing kernel. Repeated pixels remain repeated; the most common input level still contributes two of four output pixels.)
\alttext(Three nodes show the four input pixels, their inclusive cumulative counts and the resulting grayscale values 0,0,128,255.)

> Derive the discrete CDF-min mapping

Clamp each integer input to c(v)=min(255,max(0,v)), then count h(k) and form
C(k)=Σ(j≤k)h(j). Let C_min be the first nonzero cumulative count. For more than
one occupied level, the mathematical transformation is

$$T(k)=\operatorname{round}_{half\,up}\!\left(255\,\frac{\max(0,C(k)-C_{min})}{N-C_{min}}\right).$$
\alttext(After removing the first occupied cumulative mass, scale the remaining cumulative range to 0 through 255 and round half up.)

The code retains the legacy order of double conversion, subtraction, division,
multiplication, clipping and int(value+0.5). Counts are exact size_t values, but
converting very large counts above 2^53 can lose distinctions. This is not a
certified rational implementation. For the bounded examples here, the explicit
counts permit independent exact checks.

C is nondecreasing, so clipping, positive scaling and half-up conversion give a
nondecreasing lookup. In exact arithmetic nonconstant occupied endpoints map to
0 and 255. If there is only one occupied clamped level, N=C_min and the formula
has zero denominator: this implementation instead uses the identity lookup and
preserves that level. Empty shapes also use an identity lookup with zero
histograms.

The output histogram is the pushforward of the original counts:

$$h_{out}(u)=\sum_{k:T(k)=u}h(k).$$
\alttext(An output bin receives the complete mass of every input level assigned to it.)

A deterministic map cannot split equal-intensity pixels into different outputs.
Thus the number of occupied output levels cannot exceed the input count of
occupied levels. Rounding can merge levels; there is no general exact-uniformity
guarantee. The operation also ignores geometry: permuting pixel locations leaves
the histogram and lookup unchanged, and simply permutes the mapped pixels.

> Preserve the original 64×64 pattern, using one grayscale scale

The original fixture has value 96+((7i+5j)mod64). For each row, multiplication
by 5 permutes residues modulo 64, so all 64 input levels 96 through 159 occur 64
times in the full image. Its exact lookup on occupied level 96+k is
round(255k/63). Equalization reaches both output endpoints while retaining 64
occupied levels, rather than creating 256 levels.

The native image views below use the same fixed 0..255 grayscale palette.
Independently autoscaling each panel would hide the contrast change. Original
row 0 remains at the top; plot panel dimensions determine displayed cell shape.
These are controlled synthetic pixels, with no claim about photographic detail,
perceptual quality or a real noise process.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/equalization_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto input = dm::algorithms::equalization_pattern();
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::histogram_equalize_trace(input);
    const auto occupied_in =
        std::count_if(t.histogram.begin(), t.histogram.end(), [](auto n) {
        return n != 0;
    });
    const auto occupied_out =
        std::count_if(t.output_histogram.begin(), t.output_histogram.end(), [](auto n) {
        return n != 0;
    });
    std::print("Original shape {}x{}; pixels {}; occupied input levels {}; occupied output levels {}; " "cdf_min {}.\n",
               t.rows, t.columns, t.pixels, occupied_in, occupied_out, t.cdf_min);
    std::print("Actual occupied endpoint maps: 96 -> {}; 159 -> {}. Histogram pixel visits {}; output pixel " "visits {}; CDF bins {}; map bins {}.\n",
               t.mapping[96], t.mapping[159], t.work.histogram_pixels,
               t.work.output_pixels, t.work.cdf_bins, t.work.mapping_bins);
    for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k)
        if (t.histogram[96 + k] != 64)
            throw std::runtime_error("original level counts");
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
    layout.add(dm::algorithms::equalization_image_plot(input,
                                                       "Original: levels 96..159")).add(dm::algorithms::equalization_image_plot(t.image, "Mapped: fixed 0..255 scale"));
    layout.size(1100, 620).title("Same synthetic pixels, before and after the actual lookup").fit_panels().save_svg("build/equalization_original.svg");
    if (occupied_out != 64 || t.mapping[96] != 0 || t.mapping[159] != 255)
        throw std::runtime_error("original mapping");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Original shape 64x64; pixels 4096; occupied input levels 64; occupied output levels 64; cdf_min 64.
Actual occupied endpoint maps: 96 -> 0; 159 -> 255. Histogram pixel visits 4096; output pixel visits 4096; CDF bins 256; map bins 256.
\image(figures/equalization_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Both panels use fixed grayscale limits 0 and 255. The output spreads 64 distinct levels across the range; it does not become a 256-level uniform image.)
\alttext(Side-by-side native grayscale grids show the original narrow-range diagonal modular pattern and its remapped high-span values on a shared intensity scale.)

> Coarse uniformity can hide discrete masses

The original report compared 16 bins of width 16. The following table preserves
that comparison: before mapping, only four bins are populated; after mapping,
all 16 have count 256. Yet the fine output histogram still has only 64 nonzero
bins, each of mass 64. Coarse bin uniformity is therefore a property of that
summary and fixture, not a proof of a 256-level uniform distribution.

A second input contains 100 once, 101 once and 102 one thousand times. The first
two levels both map to 0, while 102 maps to 255. Three occupied input levels
become two occupied output levels. This concrete merge also shows why
deterministic equalization cannot create new intensity information.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/equalization_discrete.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto original =
        dm::algorithms::histogram_equalize_trace(dm::algorithms::equalization_pattern());
    const auto before =
        dm::algorithms::equalization_hist16(original.histogram), after =
        dm::algorithms::equalization_hist16(original.output_histogram);
    std::print("| coarse bin | levels | original count | mapped count |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int k = 0; k < 16; ++k) {
        std::print("| {} | {}..{} | {} | {} |\n", k, 16 * k, 16 * k + 15,
                   before[k], after[k]);
        if (after[k] != 256)
            throw std::runtime_error("coarse fixture");
    }
    dm::algorithms::EqualizationImage rare(1, std::vector<int>(1002, 102));
    rare[0][0] = 100;
    rare[0][1] = 101;
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::histogram_equalize_trace(rare);
    std::print("\nRare-level actual maps: 100 -> {}; 101 -> {}; 102 -> {}. Output masses at0/255: {}/{}.\n",
               t.mapping[100], t.mapping[101], t.mapping[102],
               t.output_histogram[0], t.output_histogram[255]);
    std::vector<double> b(before.begin(), before.end()), a(after.begin(),
                                                           after.end());
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    for (int k = 0; k < 16; ++k)
        labels.push_back(std::to_string(k));
    auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
    auto left =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(b, labels, "original", {148, 163, 184});
    left.title("Original 16-bin counts").x_label("bin index, width 16").y_label(
        "pixels").y_limits(0, 1400);
    auto right =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(a, labels, "mapped", {37, 99, 235});
    right.title("Mapped 16-bin counts").x_label("bin index, width 16").y_label(
        "pixels").y_limits(0, 1400);
    layout.add(left).add(right).size(1200, 620).title("Coarse bins look uniform while fine-level masses remain discrete").fit_panels().save_svg("build/equalization_discrete.svg");
    if (t.mapping[100] != 0 || t.mapping[101] != 0 ||
        t.output_histogram[255] != 1000)
        throw std::runtime_error("rare level merge");
}
