| case | rows | columns | pixels | identity lookup | clamped input locations |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | true | 0 |
| 2 | 2 | 0 | 0 | true | 0 |
| 3 | 1 | 2 | 2 | true | 0 |
| 4 | 1 | 2 | 2 | false | 2 |
Ragged shape rejected before caps true; one-short row cap rejects true; one-short pixel cap rejects true; exact caps retain 4 pixels.
\image(figures/equalization_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The diagonal is the actual constant-image lookup, with the occupied level marked. Caps separately count complete row objects and pixels; zero disables each optional cap.)
\alttext(A diagonal identity map keeps level 7 unchanged; a table separately covers empty, zero-width, constant and clamped inputs.)

The diagnostic entry point defaults to one million rows and one million pixels,
each zero disabling that policy. Every row width and checked rows×columns
product is validated before caps and output allocation. Legacy
`histogram_equalize` has no optional caps but performs the same shape/geometry
checks. Inputs are never mutated. Output rows and all fixed 256-entry tables own
their values and copy independently; calls share no mutable state.

Selected actual work is N histogram pixel visits, 256 CDF bins, 256 lookup
entries and N output visits; clamped_pixels counts out-of-range input locations
only once. Validation, allocation, copies and diagnostic bookkeeping are outside
those selected counts. Total work is O(rows+N+256), output storage O(rows+N),
and diagnostic storage fixed in the 256-level alphabet. No unbounded per-pixel
history is retained or replayed. Image plots have their own complete
display-cell cap and use one native polygon per displayed pixel, so they are
intended for modest grids rather than huge raster files.

> What this establishes

CDF equalization is an ordered global remapping, with a specific quantized and
constant-input policy. The original synthetic example spreads its range and
gives uniform coarse bins, while exact counts show that its fine distribution
remains discrete. Rare levels can merge and constant images cannot gain new
variation. These controlled fixtures isolate the mapping's behavior; no
photographic-quality, noise-removal or medical-image inference is claimed. Local
adaptive methods, color handling and contrast limiting are outside this
implementation.

The canonical reference is the [OpenCV official histogram-equalization
tutorial](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d5/daf/tutorial_py_histogram_equalization.html),
which explains cumulative mapping and distinguishes global equalization from
CLAHE. This report derives its own count/pushforward properties and retains the
library's explicit rounding, input-clamping and constant-image conventions; it
does not assert byte-for-byte equivalence with every OpenCV path.

Verification checks complete small discrete fixtures against exact
integer/rational maps, compares valid legacy outputs with a frozen
implementation, and checks actual histogram/CDF/output counts under sanitizers.
An independent oracle checks the original 4096-pixel fixture, rare-level merging
and boundary policies. All API examples, complete programs, inspected figures
and both exports are verified before the legacy report is retired.
